spot%2F15%2Fba%2F75f23a85437b97a28c69a57840c2%2F1solstice-unit-250924-01842-crop2-f.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x2000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F15%2Fba%2F75f23a85437b97a28c69a57840c2%2F1solstice-unit-250924-01842-crop2-f.jpg" class="img" alt="Joseph Zada as Haymitch Abernathy in The Hunger Games: Sunrise on the Reaping." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Joseph Zada as Haymitch Abernathy in &lt;em&gt;The Hunger Games: Sunrise on the Reaping.&lt;/em&gt;                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Murray Close/Lionsgate                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;When Katniss Everdeen first graced our screens in 2012, &lt;em&gt;The Hunger Games&lt;/em&gt; was a juggernaut of YA proportions. Highly political, violent for a PG-13 movie, and the launchpad of several Hollywood careers, supply met demand for these adaptations of Suzanne Collins' dystopian novels.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;In the latest &lt;em&gt;Hunger Games&lt;/em&gt; film, we return to Panem with a microscopic focus on Haymitch Abernathy (Joseph Zada), first introduced to audiences in the original movie as Woody Harrelson, playing a mentor to Katniss Everdeen (Jennifer Lawrence). He was cocky and careless on the outside, drowning in trauma and drunken stupors. Back then, we learned Haymitch's story: years ago, he won the 50th Hunger Games.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Sunrise on the Reaping&lt;/em&gt; goes back in time: Here, Abernathy is a 16-year-old in District 12 beloved by his community. Abernathy's curse is that he was born on the annual reaping day, and this time, he is selected as tribute for its 50th anniversary. That means twice as many tributes (48 total) will be reaped from each district.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-6046181-100" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-6046181-100" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;His entrance into the arena is not traditional. He is forced to enter the games after physically preventing his girlfriend — Lenore Dove Baird (Whitney Peak), a descendant of the traveling Covey clan — from her death, and is sent to the Capitol as punishment. In this era of Panem, the Hunger Games are still crafted behind the scenes by producers such as Plutarch Heavensbee, well-depicted by Jesse Plemons in this film. Delivered to their deaths alongside Abernathy are Mckenna Grace as ally Maysilee Donner, Ben Wang as Wyatt Callow (who comes from a family of betters and gamblers), and Molly McCann as Louella McCoy, who acts as a sister surrogate for Abernathy.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-154514" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3000;         --source-height: 2000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x2000+0+0/resize/400/quality