ormat/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x4480+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F34%2Fdf%2F22aec2e24cc3b15da15248e56479%2F0d7a40131-1.jpg" class="img" alt="Orchestra Noir was founded in 2016 by music director Jason Ikeem Rodgers. The all-Black Atlanta-based orchestra performs classic hits spanning the genres of jazz, blues and R&amp;amp;B." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Orchestra Noir was founded in 2016 by music director Jason Ikeem Rodgers. The all-Black Atlanta-based orchestra performs classic hits spanning the genres of jazz, blues and R&amp;B.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Andre Calder                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Ashley Branch, Orchestra Noir's operations manager, hurriedly completes her final tasks before curtain call at the Warner Theater in Washington, D.C. It is two hours before the ensemble's Saturday night concert and her main goal is simple: plan to deal with whatever she didn't plan for.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The D.C. show comes partway through the ensemble's 20-city The Culture 2000s tour, which has taken the group to cities like Jacksonville and Philadelphia.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Founded in 2016 by music director Jason Ikeem Rodgers, the all-Black Atlanta-based orchestra &lt;a href="https://www.orchestranoir.com/" target="_blank"&gt;performs classic hits&lt;/a&gt; spanning the genres of jazz, blues and R&amp;B. Branch has been with Orchestra Noir for about two years, hired to bring relief to Keisha Rodgers, the orchestra's original operations manager, who now serves as president of operations. On show nights, it's Branch who is directing the final details on show prep.&lt;/p&gt;        &lt;div id="resg-s1-154644-100" class="bucketwrap video youtube-video large"&gt;     &lt;div class="video-wrap"&gt;         &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/nI2m15Y1RNo?rel=0" width="100%" height="100%" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen=""&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;         &lt;div class="credit-caption externalasset"&gt;                               &lt;b class="credit"&gt;                                          &lt;b class="source"&gt;YouTube&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;/b&gt;         &lt;/div&gt; &lt;/div&gt;   &lt;p&gt;In its early days, the Orchestra Noir team was small — just Keisha and Jason handling everything. While Jason was responsible for all music-related details, Keisha handled other tasks, such as creating the logo and website.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"I wore every single hat there was to wear," Keisha told NPR. "It was just very day-to-day. I mean, every little thing you could think of operationally was me."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;But over the last decade, Orchestra Noir has expanded to 23 musicians and a leadership staff of four people. The orchestra averages about 20 shows per tour.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Saxophonist Taj Davis and violinist Nicole Wright have been with the ensemble for three tours. They say the size of the production has grown considerably since they joined.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"Every go round is a new element," Davis said.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The latest season added several new details to their performances — including a full display screen cycling through visuals from previous performances, fog machines and lighting and choreography threaded throughout the show.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-154649" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2048;         --source-height: 1365;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2048x1365+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F61%2F13%2F075ae1b34b6799cd8d26c25076f3%2Fpromo-photo.jpeg" class="img" alt="Shondalla Calder plays the violin during Orchestra Noir performance for their The Culture 2000s tour." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Shondalla Calder plays the violin during an Orchestra Noir performance for their The Culture 2000s tour.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Andre Calder                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;"Every time I come back, I'm excited," Wright said, "because not only am I experiencing something different, but the audience is too." The theme for each tour, Rodgers explained, comes from whatever is igniting his creativity at the moment. For their most recent The Culture 2000s tour, Rodgers said they really wanted the "Bad Gal Rihanna energy."&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;"I chose the theme based on basically what inspired me at the time. And that's kind of where I want to go. I want to go deep into the 2000's," he said.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;When it comes to picking which songs to perform and how they will be arranged, Rodgers compares it to developing a secret sauce he's been trying perfect "for a long time."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"We developed the formula where we can orchestrate and we can arrange," Rogers said. "We can orchestrate for those songs and arrange those songs in a way that is still so authentic, like how we're playing the melody, which instruments should have the bass line or the the harmony." "So it's really about seeing the orchestration in each song," Rogers continued. "Now when I hear a hip-hop song or a R&amp;B song — I don't even hear the words anymore. I just hear instruments." &lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The journey to the stage&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Preparation for an Orchestra Noir show starts long before the curtain rises. Wright logs two hours of individual practice daily. She writes out compositions by hand and thinks through fingerings and lyrics before bed. She says the show's music is often top of her mind as she moves through the day, even when her violin isn't in her hands. Wright says that her intense focus on the details of the show's music ahead of the show allows her to "just let loose and become one with the audience" once showtime arrives.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-154648" class="bucketwrap image medium"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 853;         --source-height: 1280;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/c